<binaryimage arch="ARM:LE:32:v7:default">
<!--
    GH-1922 follow-up (kuna): "Calls to an in-image memcpy keep only their
    first arguments (Betaflight, DWARF)", for callers with no declaration.
    https://github.com/Noelo-Lab/kuna/issues/1922

    Reduced from Crazyflie syslinkSendPacket (Cortex-M4, gcc -O2, DWARF
    ignored), whose call to the firmware's own newlib memcpy printed two of
    its three arguments.  Hand-written Thumb-2, assembled with clang for
    thumbv7em and linked at 0x10000:

      mcpy      newlib-shaped copy: reads r0, r1 and r2, writes r3 first.
      cp4       reads r0 and r1 only.
      take      reads r0 and r1 (xSemaphoreTake).
      send_pkt  push {r3, r4, r5, r6, r7, lr}; bl take; ...
                ldrb r2,[r6,#1]; strb r2,[r5,#3]; adds r1,r6,#2;
                adds r0,r5,#4; bl mcpy (the length is also stored).
      send4     send_pkt calling cp4.  Control: cp4 does not read r2.
      vsend     push {r0, r1, r2, r3}; push {r4, r5, r6, lr}; then
                send_pkt's body.  Control: a variadic register save
                (Betaflight cliPrintf, tfp_sprintf).
      vsendz    sub sp, #12; push {r3, r4, r5, r6, r7, lr};
                add r4, sp, #24; stm r4!, {r1, r2, r3}; then
                send_pkt's body with &a0[3].  Control: clang -Oz
                reserves stack with a pushed r3 beneath lr AND saves
                r1-r3 with stm, so it is still a variadic save.

    Stage model: P4 active-input-trial scoring.  The length register is
    refused by onlyOpUse (it has another use), and the callee-read rule
    (kuna_calleereadarg.rs) declined to add it back because the caller's
    entry stores r3, its last argument register, only to save it, which the
    rule reads as a possible variadic register-save area.  A save stores the
    argument registers on their own, r3 highest, beneath the incoming stack
    arguments; a push that stores r3 beneath lr is gcc's 8-byte alignment
    pad, and no longer declines the rule.  vsend's and vsendz's saves still
    do.
-->
